5. Dyadic endpoint-debt cascade
   For a speed v, its 2v endpoints are protected by integer residues p
   satisfying |p*(16m+/-1)-a*16v| < v.  The table separates two cases:
   a super-gate p=0 mod 16v protects everything, while a maximum-speed
   branch can only use p<v.
   v   endpoints  best p<v cover  exact<=16  greedy p<v cover  first greedy protectors
   1           2               0/2           -     0 speeds cover   0/2    -
   2           4               2/4           -     1 speeds cover   2/4    1
   4           8               4/8           -     1 speeds cover   4/8    2
   8          16               8/16          -     1 speeds cover   8/16   4
   16         32              16/32          9     9 speeds cover  32/32   8,1,3,5,7,9,11,13
       exact lower cover for v=16: (1, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9, 11, 13, 15)
   32         64              32/64          -     9 speeds cover  64/64   16,1,3,5,7,9,11,13
   64        128              64/128         -    11 speeds cover 128/128  32,1,5,6,7,11,13,27
   128       256             128/256         -    13 speeds cover 256/256  64,1,9,10,11,14,4,34
   Read this as a possible proof spine: a largest 16-divisible gate
   cannot use a super-gate, so it asks for half-gate residues; those
   ask for quarter-gates; the unit layer asks to be rescued by a
   16-gate.  This is a finite dyadic debt cycle, not a linear cover.

