   dyadic endpoints.

7. Proof synthesis after the failed attacks
   Candidate theorem A (proved here): no 16-gate implies no open cover,
   because odd unit points a/16 survive as boundary witnesses.
   Candidate theorem B (new target): every primitive n=16 full-measure
   set containing a 16-gate has a nonempty dyadic endpoint-debt leaf.
   Equivalently, the endpoint-protection core peels to empty before an
   arithmetic cycle can close.
   The wild but now concrete route is a dyadic debt-flow inequality:
   charge endpoints by their layer 1,2,4,8,16; half-turn symmetry makes
   odd protectors one-sided; maximum-speed endpoints cannot use a
   super-gate; and the debt cycle must eventually ask the original
   16-gate to rescue the unit layer.  Show that this directed debt
   cycle has positive divergence for only 15 speeds.
   The Cayley-Dickson analogy becomes precise here: the sedenion row is
   exactly where division fails, and in LRC that failure is endpoint
   debt leaking one dyadic layer downward every time a gate closes an
   older boundary.
